WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.180
<v Maya>Welcome to part five.

2
00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:11.070
<v Maya>Last time we met Sumit, twenty years old, maximum heart rate two hundred, resting heart rate eighty, a beginner at fifty to sixty percent.

3
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:19.300
<v Maya>Today we turn that percentage into a target heart rate, we see why a lower resting heart rate is better, and we look at the devices that monitor heart rate.

4
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:26.620
<v Maya>A target heart rate, THR, can be a single number, like one fifty five, or a range, like one fifty to one sixty.

5
00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.560
<v Maya>Which makes more sense, Dev?

6
00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:30.150
<v Dev>A range, I'd guess.

7
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.170
<v Dev>Heart rate isn't fixed.

8
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.070
<v Maya>Right.

9
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.270
<v Maya>It fluctuates by a few beats, like room temperature.

10
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.730
<v Maya>Over twenty minutes you can reach one fifty five, but maintaining exactly that is impossible.

11
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.830
<v Maya>A range says, don't go below this, don't exceed that.

12
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.360
<v Maya>Think of buying a shirt.

13
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:53.850
<v Maya>Walk into a high end store and ask for a shirt of exactly four thousand eight hundred and thirty two rupees, and you'll be shown the door.

14
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.760
<v Maya>You say, between four and five thousand.

15
00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.830
<v Dev>So we calculate twice.

16
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.220
<v Maya>Yes, in both methods, once for the lower limit and once for the upper limit.

17
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:10.050
<v Maya>Method one is the age predicted method, also called the MHR method, because it uses maximum heart rate.

18
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:17.840
<v Maya>The formula is THR equals maximum heart rate times intensity, so two hundred and twenty minus age, times intensity.

19
00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:22.390
<v Maya>Same method, two names, so don't be confused if an exam uses either.

20
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.060
<v Dev>Sumit's numbers?

21
00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:30.600
<v Maya>Lower limit, two twenty minus twenty is two hundred, times fifty over a hundred, which is one hundred beats per minute.

22
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:35.280
<v Maya>Upper limit, two hundred times sixty over a hundred, which is one hundred and twenty.

23
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.530
<v Maya>So the range is one hundred to one hundred and twenty.

24
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.570
<v Maya>His resting heart rate of eighty isn't used at all.

25
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.460
<v Dev>Does that make sense for a guy at eighty resting?

26
00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:45.810
<v Maya>That's the loophole.

27
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.420
<v Maya>Follow Sumit's day.

28
00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:48.860
<v Maya>He wakes at eighty.

29
00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:54.670
<v Maya>After his morning routine, a ten minute walk to the gym and three floors by stairs, he's about ninety.

30
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.430
<v Maya>After the gym tour, mobility drills and waiting at the treadmill, about ninety five.

31
00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.590
<v Maya>So cardio starts at ninety five.

32
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:08.080
<v Maya>If he just walks casually and chats with a friend, within two minutes he's in the one hundred to one hundred and twenty zone.

33
00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.850
<v Maya>Does that look like fifty percent intensity?

34
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.780
<v Dev>No, that's barely effort.

35
00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:13.910
<v Maya>Exactly.

36
00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.190
<v Maya>The method only considers age.

37
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.410
<v Maya>It never considers current fitness level, which the resting heart rate shows.

38
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:26.830
<v Maya>All twenty year old beginners would get the same range, but one may be near sixty, one at seventy, one at eighty.

39
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.920
<v Maya>Like three friends, all thirty years old, and a salesman who gives all three a thirty six waist jeans.

40
00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:36.560
<v Maya>Body composition isn't the same for all thirty year olds.

41
00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.170
<v Maya>We need a customised target.

42
00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:45.960
<v Maya>That's why Karvonen, an exercise physiologist, came up with the heart rate reserve method, also called Karvonen's formula.

43
00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:50.940
<v Maya>THR equals heart rate reserve times intensity, plus resting heart rate.

44
00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:57.260
<v Dev>So before we multiplied the maximum, now we multiply the reserve and add resting rate back.

45
00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.150
<v Maya>Yes.

46
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.800
<v Maya>To make it ready to use, open it up in steps.

47
00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.850
<v Maya>Heart rate reserve is maximum minus resting.

48
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.420
<v Maya>And maximum is two twenty minus age.

49
00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:13.010
<v Maya>So THR equals, two twenty minus age minus resting rate, times intensity, plus resting rate.

50
00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.450
<v Dev>Sumit again.

51
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:15.640
<v Maya>Lower limit.

52
00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.980
<v Maya>Two twenty minus twenty minus eighty.

53
00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.340
<v Maya>Brackets first.

54
00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:24.010
<v Maya>Two twenty minus twenty is two hundred, minus eighty is one hundred and twenty.

55
00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.490
<v Maya>Then multiplication before addition.

56
00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:30.780
<v Maya>One twenty times fifty percent is half of one twenty, which is sixty.

57
00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.100
<v Maya>Then add eighty.

58
00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:33.790
<v Maya>One hundred and forty.

59
00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.650
<v Maya>Upper limit, one twenty times sixty percent is seventy two, plus eighty is one hundred and fifty two.

60
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.600
<v Maya>So the range is one forty to one fifty two.

61
00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.420
<v Dev>Why does the order matter?

62
00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.800
<v Maya>Think of the letters BODMAS.

63
00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:51.150
<v Maya>Brackets, order, division, multiplication, addition, subtraction.

64
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.790
<v Maya>If you jumped to one twenty plus eighty first, your answer changes entirely.

65
00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.640
<v Dev>So the same client went from one hundred to one twenty, to one forty to one fifty two.

66
00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:04.910
<v Maya>Yes, and the new lower limit is above the old upper limit.

67
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.580
<v Maya>The age predicted method uses age only, and gives a generalised range.

68
00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:14.650
<v Maya>The Karvonen method uses age and resting heart rate, and gives a customised one.

69
00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.420
<v Maya>This is the method accepted worldwide.

70
00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:27.280
<v Dev>One thing, if you convert one forty to one fifty two back into percent of maximum, that's about seventy to seventy six percent, which looks like the high band.

71
00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.230
<v Maya>The notes flag that, and the lecture doesn't discuss it.

72
00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:35.620
<v Maya>In this method the fifty to sixty percent is applied to the reserve, not to the maximum.

73
00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:42.110
<v Maya>The E-book also uses forty to sixty percent of reserve for initial conditioning, so check your course material.

74
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.930
<v Maya>Now why is a lower resting heart rate better.

75
00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:51.220
<v Maya>Heart rate reserve is a range for the heart to go up, from resting, eighty for Sumit, to maximum, two hundred.

76
00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.540
<v Maya>Like a reserve fuel tank in a vehicle.

77
00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:55.430
<v Maya>The bigger the reserve, the better.

78
00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:59.130
<v Maya>If three litres can't reach a petrol pump, five litres helps.

79
00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.520
<v Maya>Or extra cash for a metro traveller.

80
00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.340
<v Maya>With a hundred rupees you can take a cab home.

81
00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:07.590
<v Maya>With five hundred you can also have a meal and a coffee.

82
00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.130
<v Dev>And in the heart?

83
00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.630
<v Maya>If the heart rate gets very close to maximum during work, you feel exhausted.

84
00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:20.860
<v Maya>If Sumit's resting rate were seventy instead of eighty, the same work leaves him further from exhaustion, because he has a bigger tank.

85
00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:26.880
<v Maya>Mathematically, reserve is maximum minus resting, so a lower resting rate widens the reserve.

86
00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.760
<v Maya>Maximum can't be changed, it depends on age.

87
00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:33.840
<v Maya>But resting heart rate can be worked on, because cardio reduces it.

88
00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:35.370
<v Dev>Is there a story?

89
00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:36.740
<v Maya>The guitar class.

90
00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:42.160
<v Maya>Sumit finishes college at four, and his guitar class is at four fifteen, up some stairs.

91
00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:53.910
<v Maya>With resting rate eighty, he reaches the building at four twenty five, exhausted, sits on the stairs gasping, arrives in class at four thirty five, recovers by four forty, and a track has already been taught.

92
00:05:53.960 --> 00:06:05.510
<v Maya>After three months of cardio, resting rate seventy, he reaches the building around four twenty, climbs without a break, waits briefly on the third floor at four twenty five, and arrives at four twenty eight.

93
00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:12.940
<v Maya>After three more months, resting rate sixty, he arrives at four ten, needs no break, and is seated at four fifteen sharp.

94
00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.700
<v Dev>So cardio shows up in daily life.

95
00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:16.830
<v Maya>Exactly.

96
00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:24.110
<v Maya>Now for three clients with resting rates eighty, seventy and sixty, call them A, B and C, all with maximum two hundred.

97
00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.590
<v Maya>If all got one forty to one fifty two, that would be unfair.

98
00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:31.070
<v Maya>The notes computed them with the same formula, not in the lecture.

99
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.560
<v Maya>Client B, reserve one thirty, range one thirty five to one forty eight.

100
00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:39.880
<v Maya>Client C, reserve one forty, range one thirty to one forty four.

101
00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.400
<v Maya>Customised, valid and accurate.

102
00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:43.630
<v Dev>Recap?

103
00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.540
<v Maya>Range, not a single number.

104
00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:51.650
<v Maya>Age predicted, two twenty minus age times intensity, Sumit one hundred to one hundred and twenty.

105
00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:55.090
<v Maya>Karvonen, brackets first, one forty to one fifty two.

106
00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.520
<v Maya>Lower resting rate means bigger reserve.

107
00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:00.290
<v Maya>Once Sumit has his range, how does he check it?

108
00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.140
<v Maya>There are three types of electronic devices.

109
00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:04.330
<v Dev>First?

110
00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.880
<v Maya>The chest strap, known for high accuracy.

111
00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:12.120
<v Maya>The main unit sits just above the sternum, closest to the heart, with electrodes behind it.

112
00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:19.040
<v Maya>The electrodes receive the heart's electric signals, and the heart rate shows on a phone or watch connected by Bluetooth.

113
00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:22.080
<v Maya>Athletes need this almost real time accuracy.

114
00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:24.600
<v Maya>Brands, Wahoo, Garmin, Polar.

115
00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:26.260
<v Dev>And the smartwatch?

116
00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:27.890
<v Maya>Known for convenience.

117
00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:30.210
<v Maya>Rings use the same technology.

118
00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:32.190
<v Maya>Accuracy varies with price.

119
00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.320
<v Maya>Basic models give calories, steps and heart rate.

120
00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:39.980
<v Maya>Expensive ones add VO₂ max, sleep quality, GPS and more.

121
00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:42.060
<v Maya>Mostly used by gym goers.

122
00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.830
<v Maya>Brands, Apple, Samsung, Fitbit, Garmin, and cheaper ones like Xiaomi.

123
00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:48.970
<v Dev>How does it measure heart rate?

124
00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.560
<v Maya>Photoplethysmography, PPG.

125
00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:56.390
<v Maya>On the back of the watch, some circles are LED lights and some are light sensors.

126
00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:59.350
<v Maya>The LED lights send light into the body.

127
00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:03.130
<v Maya>When the heart beats, blood flow is higher, so more light is absorbed.

128
00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:08.120
<v Maya>Between two beats, flow drops, so less is absorbed and more is reflected back.

129
00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:13.370
<v Maya>The sensors capture the reflected light, and the watch analyses it and shows the heart rate.

130
00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:14.770
<v Dev>And the third?

131
00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:17.980
<v Maya>Grip sensors, the built in monitors on gym machines.

132
00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.480
<v Maya>You put your fingers in holes on the handles.

133
00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:22.920
<v Maya>They're not recommended, for three reasons.

134
00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:27.680
<v Maya>Weak signals, because each beat creates only weak electric signals on the skin.

135
00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:32.450
<v Maya>Motion interference, because contact can't be maintained when jogging or running.

136
00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:38.480
<v Maya>And salt accumulation, because sweat salt and sanitiser leave an invisible barrier on the sensor.

137
00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:42.400
<v Dev>What do I say to a client who refuses to buy a watch?

138
00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:47.400
<v Maya>That even a basic smartwatch is not an overhead expense, it's an investment in themselves.

139
00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:48.600
<v Maya>Pause and think.

140
00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:51.720
<v Maya>Sumit's resting rate is eighty and age twenty.

141
00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:54.930
<v Maya>What is the Karvonen upper limit at sixty percent?

142
00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:00.920
<v Dev>Reserve is one twenty, times point six is seventy two, plus eighty is one fifty two.

143
00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:01.920
<v Maya>Correct.

144
00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:03.980
<v Maya>Here are the five things to remember.

145
00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:07.810
<v Maya>One, give a target range, calculated twice, lower and upper.

146
00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:13.450
<v Maya>Two, age predicted, two twenty minus age times intensity, ignores resting heart rate.

147
00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:19.920
<v Maya>Three, Karvonen, two twenty minus age minus resting, times intensity, plus resting, brackets first.

148
00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:24.450
<v Maya>Four, lower resting heart rate means bigger reserve, and cardio lowers it.

149
00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:29.920
<v Maya>Five, chest strap most accurate, smartwatch convenient, grip sensors not recommended.

150
00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:34.810
<v Dev>Next, estimating intensity without devices, and training methods.

151
00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.140
<v Maya>See you in part six.

